r.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 3: Planning a schedule","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Time","week":31,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA film starts at 4:20 and lasts 95 minutes → ends about 5:55. If you must leave at 5:45, you do not have enough time to stay to the end.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Planning with start and duration.","caption":"Watch hour rollover.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Film</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">4:20 + 95 min</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">End</text><tex